  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14193271/assets/ac5d02edc0945574b5bf8f9a1112e9d7e9ca33ebc7714bfadcd14d02e46c43c7.png)\n\nFor the pentagon above, determine the measure of the exterior angle of $∠A.$",
  "worked_solution": "Note that the measure of the exterior angle at $C$ is ${180}^{∘}-(4x-{1}^{∘})={181}^{∘}-4x,$ and the measure of the exterior angle at $E$ is ${180}^{∘}-{86}^{∘}={94}^{∘}.$\n\nSince the sum of the exterior angles of a polygon is ${360}^{∘},$  we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x+{1}^{∘})+(2x+{14}^{∘})+({181}^{∘}-4x)+(2x+{16}^{∘})+{94}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ (2x+2x-4x+2x)+({1}^{∘}+{14}^{∘}+{181}^{∘}+{16}^{∘}+{94}^{∘}) & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ 2x+{306}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ 2x & ={54}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={27}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the measure of the exterior angle of $∠A$ equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+{1}^{∘} & =2({27}^{∘})+{1}^{∘} \\\\  & ={55}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
